Almost any seasoned RPG player will raise such issues.  In 
pen-and-paper surroundings, players who want to dive deeper 
into the setting usually want to be able to find the actions 
taking place and their results plausible to some degree once 
you accept some specific axioms, like the existence of magic, 
gods, etc.  Of course, this does not mean they want a closed 
Unified Magic Field Theory, but be able to build up some kind 
of belief in the consistency of the game world.  

One may find it amusing that such reflexes spring on even 
when one plays a computer game, where clearly no such level 
of 'pseudo-realism' is attainable nor desirable ("you feel 
29 corpses of long worms here", "There's a grey dragon corpse 
here; do you want to tin it (y/n)?"); however, if it would 
increase peoples' playing satisfaction, it is perfectly 
legitimate to ask for a little more 'realism' here and there, 
provided this won't hurt other player's fun.  

